**Abstract class.** A class that exists to be a base class and cannot be instantiated directly. It defines a common interface (and possibly some shared implementation) that derived classes must complete. In C++ a class becomes abstract when it has at least one pure virtual function. In Java/C# you mark it with the `abstract` keyword and it may contain both abstract and concrete methods.

**Pure virtual function.** A virtual function declared with no required implementation in the base class, written with `= 0`:

```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() const = 0;   // pure virtual
    virtual ~Shape() = default;        // virtual destructor
    void describe() const { /* concrete, shared code */ }
};

class Circle : public Shape {
    double r;
public:
    Circle(double r) : r(r) {}
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; }
};

Shape* s = new Circle(2);   // OK: base pointer, derived object
// Shape x;                 // error: cannot instantiate abstract class
```

**Rules to mention.**
- A derived class must override every pure virtual function, otherwise it is also abstract.
- You can still hold pointers or references to the abstract type; calls are dispatched at runtime through the vtable, which is how runtime polymorphism works.
- A pure virtual function may optionally have a body (defined outside the class). This is commonly done for a pure virtual destructor, which must have a definition.
- Always give a polymorphic base class a virtual destructor, so deleting through a base pointer runs the derived destructor.

**Why use it.** It enforces a contract (every `Shape` has `area()`), lets code depend on the abstraction rather than concrete types, and supports the open/closed principle.

**Compare with an interface.** C++ has no `interface` keyword; a class with only pure virtual functions plays that role. Java has interfaces and abstract classes: an abstract class can hold state and constructors, while an interface mainly declares behaviour.

